---
/*
  ACHIEVEMENTS STRIP — restores the round-1 stats-row presentation the
  owner explicitly liked (small uppercase intro label, several big
  numbers side by side, each with one short context line, staggered
  reveal) after round 2's single-fact "capability strips" misread the
  direction. Extended, per the round-3 correction, to repeat that exact
  presentation ONCE PER CASE STUDY within one shared section — each
  block gets its own heading + one-line subline, its own stats, and a
  closing inline link out to where the full story lives.

  ROUND 6 (superseded): bright, saturated, theme-invariant fills
  (brand-300, gold-400, slate-400) with fixed dark text.

  ROUND 7 (2026-08-26) — depth/refinement pass. Owner direction: the
  case studies should feel friendlier — "white with a bit of gloss" —
  and the page shouldn't read flat. The panels are now raised, top-lit
  cards that follow the theme again (white + gloss in light theme, an
  elevated lit surface in dark theme), with each case study keeping
  its color identity via tone-accent stat bars ONLY — a tone-accent
  top bar was tried in this round and rejected by the owner
  ("תעלים אותו לגמרי"), so the panel's top edge is just the inset
  catch-light. All of that lives in the
  `.strip-panel*` / `.strip-stat` classes in global.css (see there for
  the token setup and the AA contrast math); text here goes back to
  the `text-text-primary`/`text-text-muted` theme tokens since the
  fill follows the theme again.

  COMPOSITION — also per round 6: the owner wants "a smooth and compact
  section," explicitly describing the goal as "one flowing row of
  proof," not "three heavy slabs." Read that as a literal cue, not just
  a metaphor for tighter spacing: the 3 strips now sit in a genuine
  3-column grid at sm+ (stacking to 1 column below that), not a
  vertically stacked list of full-width panels. This is the single
  biggest lever for "more compact" — three panels side-by-side take
  roughly a third of the vertical height three stacked panels do, far
  more than any padding/gap tightening alone could achieve. Within each
  (now much narrower) panel, stats stack in a simple single column
  instead of their own sub-grid — a 3-across sub-grid inside an already
  1/3-width panel would be too cramped to read. Padding, gaps, heading
  size, and stat-value size are all tightened again on top of this
  (see the values below vs round 5's).

  Every stat value must be traceable to a real source (see CLAUDE.md,
  Content honesty) — never invent or round a number to make it look
  better. As of round 6 (the Reception strip's deletion, an explicit
  owner decision — see index.astro's PAGE GOAL comment), there are NO
  placeholder values anywhere on this page; the [OWNER NUMBER NEEDED]
  exception that existed through round 5 no longer applies to anything
  rendered by this component.

  ROUND 8 (2026-08-26) — declutter pass. Owner verdict on the round-7
  panels: "עמוס, צפוף לי. לא ברור" (busy, cramped, unclear). Four
  causes, four fixes — NO copy changed:
    1. No focal element: the stat value (text-lg/xl) sat one type step
       from the heading (text-base) — nothing owned the card. Values
       are now text-2xl; everything else is subordinated to them.
    2. Whitespace starvation: p-4 panels, 10px between stat rows whose
       descriptions wrap 3-4 lines, mt-3 between every zone — a text
       wall. Panel padding is p-5 (global.css), subline→stats mt-5,
       16px between stat rows, value→description mt-1, link mt-auto.
    3. Accent-bar fragmentation: three per-stat bars of unequal height
       per card (nine ticks across the section) chopped each card into
       mini-boxes and fought the panel-edge alignment of heading/link.
       Replaced by ONE continuous group bar (`.strip-stats`) — one
       indent, one clear "numbers zone".
    4. Premature 3-col: the grid split at sm (640px) → ~185px columns.
       It now splits at md; below that the cards stack full-width.

  ROUND 9 (2026-08-26) — owner: "תעלים גם את הצבעים השונים של הפסים
  בתוך הכרטיסים. שיהיה אחיד." The per-case-study tone colors on the
  group bars (brand/gold/slate) are gone — every card's bar is now the
  same brand accent, set directly in `.strip-stats` (global.css). The
  `tone` prop stays in the API (both homepages still pass it) but is
  visually inert — nothing maps it to a class anymore.

  ROUND 10 (2026-08-27) — owner: "במקום הפס הצבעוני בסטאדיז, שיהיה
  נקודות... נקודה לכל מספר כמובן ושישאר מיושר אותו הדבר." The round
  8-9 continuous group bar (`.strip-stats`) is replaced by one small
  brand dot per stat row (`.strip-stat` in global.css, a ::before
  marker centered on the value's first line). The start indent is
  byte-identical to the bar's (18px), so the card geometry and gap
  rhythm are unchanged; RTL mirroring via logical properties as
  before. Same uniform brand accent in both themes as the bar had.

  STAT-CELL LOOK — round-1 DNA preserved in spirit (extrabold value,
  staggered `--reveal-delay`); each stat row carries its accent
  marker via `.strip-stat` in global.css.

  CASCADE-LAYER NOTE: `.strip-stat` is
  defined after `.strip-panel` in global.css (same reasoning as
  `.card-row`/`.card-compact`) so they win by source order within the
  same compiled utilities layer. The theme-token text utilities on the
  heading/value/description/link have no competing component-class
  base setting `color` on those elements — no cascade risk stacking
  them directly. The closing link still uses `.focus-ring` +
  `.focus-ring-on-900` (a small, accepted mismatch against the panel
  fill, unchanged reasoning from round 5).

  LINK, NOT A STRETCHED CARD: each panel has real content beyond the
  link (heading, subline, several stats) — a plain inline text link at
  the end reads more honestly than making the numbers themselves seem
  clickable. Underlined (since round 5) rather than color-differentiated
  so it doesn't rely on color alone to read as interactive (WCAG 1.4.1);
  round 7 moves the link to the theme text tokens (muted at rest,
  primary on hover) since the panel fill follows the theme again —
  both directions verified AA in global.css's `.strip-panel` comment.

  PROPS:
    intro?  — small label above all strips; doubles as this section's
              <h2> (same pattern as round 2's landmark fix) — omit only
              if the copy genuinely has no intro line.
    strips  — array of case-study blocks:
                heading — case-study label (e.g. "A family
                          fuel-distribution business")
                subline — one line of context under the heading
                stats   — array of { value, description } pairs
                href       — link target (case study or product page)
                linkLabel  — visible link text (e.g. "Read the full story")
                tone?      — "brand" | "gold" | "slate". VISUALLY INERT
                             since round 9 (owner uniformity decision —
                             all bars are the brand accent now); kept
                             in the API so existing callers don't
                             break and the per-strip identity survives
                             in data if the owner ever reverses.
    note?   — optional small closing line under all strips.
    id?     — optional anchor id.
    flushBottom? — drop this section's bottom border so an immediately
              following section that shares `bg-surface-900` and draws
              its own `border-b` (the HE homepage's QuotesStrip) merges
              with it into one continuous bordered block. Default false
              — the EN homepage keeps the self-contained border-y frame.
*/
interface Stat {
  value:       string;
  description: string;
}

type Tone = "brand" | "gold" | "slate";

interface Strip {
  heading:   string;
  subline:   string;
  stats:     Stat[];
  href:      string;
  linkLabel: string;
  tone?:     Tone;
}

interface Props {
  intro?: string;
  strips: Strip[];
  note?:  string;
  id?:    string;
  flushBottom?: boolean;
}

const { intro, strips, note, id, flushBottom = false } = Astro.props;
---

<section id={id} class:list={[flushBottom ? "border-t" : "border-y", "border-border-subtle bg-surface-900 px-6 py-section-sm"]}>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl">

    <!-- Intro label — doubles as the section's <h2> landmark -->
    {intro && (
      <h2 class="reveal text-center text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-text-muted">
        {intro}
      </h2>
    )}

    <!-- THREE-ACROSS ROW OF PROOF (round 6) — a genuine grid, not a stacked list, is the main lever for "compact"; 1 column on mobile, 3 at md+ (round 8: was sm+, but 640px/3 gave ~185px columns — a "cramped" cause in its own right). -->
    <div class:list={["grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 md:grid-cols-3 md:gap-5", intro && "mt-8"]}>
      {strips.map((strip, i) => {
        return (
          <article class="reveal flex flex-col strip-panel" style={`--reveal-delay: ${i * 100}`}>

            <!-- Heading bumped text-sm → text-base (owner feedback: "תגדיל את הפונט מעט גם בכותרת של הקייס סטאדיז"). -->
            <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-text-primary">
              {strip.heading}
            </h3>
            <p class="mt-1.5 text-xs leading-relaxed text-text-muted">
              {strip.subline}
            </p>

            <!-- STATS — stacked single column (each panel is only ~1/3 width now, too narrow for its own sub-grid). Each row is a .strip-stat: a small brand dot per stat, centered on the value's first line (round 10, owner: "נקודה לכל מספר"; replaces round 8-9's continuous group bar at the identical 18px start indent, so the alignment is unchanged). Values are the card's focal element: text-2xl, everything else quieter. -->
            <div class="mt-5 flex flex-col gap-4">
              {strip.stats.map((stat, j) => (
                <div
                  class="reveal strip-stat"
                  style={`--reveal-delay: ${i * 100 + j * 60}`}
                >
                  <p class="text-2xl font-extrabold leading-tight tracking-tight text-text-primary">
                    {stat.value}
                  </p>
                  <p class="mt-1 text-xs leading-relaxed text-text-muted">
                    {stat.description}
                  </p>
                </div>
              ))}
            </div>

            <!--
              CLOSING LINK — plain inline text link, not a stretched card
              target. Theme-token colors, underlined (round 7 — underline,
              not color, signals "interactive").
              `py-1 -my-1` widens the tap target a little on mobile
              without adding visible gap (negative margin cancels it).
              mt-auto (round 8): the three cards' content heights
              differ, so the links now pin to the panel bottom and
              align across the row instead of floating at three
              different heights.
            -->
            <p class="mt-auto pt-5 text-xs">
              <a
                href={strip.href}
                class="focus-ring focus-ring-on-900 group -my-1 inline-flex items-center gap-1 rounded-sm py-1 font-medium text-text-muted underline decoration-text-muted/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-text-primary hover:decoration-text-primary/70"
              >
                {strip.linkLabel}
                <!-- rtl:rotate-180 (HE mirror fix): resting arrow must point toward line-start (left in RTL); previously only the hover nudge direction flipped, leaving the glyph pointing the wrong way at rest in RTL. -->
                <svg class="h-3.5 w-3.5 rtl:rotate-180 transition-transform group-hover:translate-x-1 rtl:group-hover:-translate-x-1" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" aria-hidden="true">
                  <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M9 5l7 7-7 7" />
                </svg>
              </a>
            </p>

          </article>
        );
      })}
    </div>

    {note && (
      <p class="reveal mt-6 text-center text-xs text-text-muted">
        {note}
      </p>
    )}

  </div>
</section>
